= Context
The Academy of Integrated Science (AIS) is committed to integrating STEM disciplines by designing interdisciplinary curricula and growing our enrollment. These efforts rely heavily on both strategic recruitment and continuous curricular innovation. AIS dedicates significant resources to recruitment and retention, notably through the work of Sharon Stacy, who coordinates with the College and partners such as Sam Hannah to raise interest in our programs among prospective students. 

However, these investments can only achieve their full impact if they are paired with energetic and ongoing curricular innovation. AIS collaborates extensively with departments across the College of Science and seeks further collaboration with other colleges to develop integrated programs that demonstrate the interconnectedness of STEM fields. This is central to the mission of AIS—evidenced by initiatives like our Integrated Science Curriculum, the Nanoscience and Nanomedicine majors, and the emerging Science, Technology, and Law program.

A key structural point is that AIS itself is home to \emph{collegiate faculty}, whereas tenure-line faculty must hold appointments in standard academic departments. Collegiate faculty lines are especially well suited for championing curricular development across departmental boundaries. In AIS, I work diligently to support our collegiate faculty (and affiliated faculty in other departments) with resources and mentorship. This support has paid off: our programs have strong student outcomes, and we frequently hear how our integrative approach helps students find their career paths and discover new ambitions—one of the core purposes of higher education.

Nevertheless, we must act both strategically and responsibly to ensure our programs remain robust. As our student numbers grow, it is critical to preserve and enhance the high-quality experience that AIS is known for. Preserving the student experience includes offering more diverse electives, implementing multiple pathways to meet requirements, and expanding pedagogical methods—efforts that require sufficient numbers of faculty. 

Recent and upcoming changes heighten this need. AIS had two unsuccessful searches in Nanomedicine in previous years due to hiring challenges in the aftermath of the Covid pandemic; we also had a hiring request denied last year. We plan to extend the Nanomedicine program, broaden the Science, Technology, and Law minor, and are discussing a future M.S. in Behavior Decision Sciences. Additionally, we face the departure of Dr. Chixia Tian this summer, who has been our sole AIS collegiate faculty member heavily involved in teaching Nanomedicine courses that are required for our majors. 

These factors underscore the urgency for new hires. Without them, there is a risk of overburdening current faculty, curtailing curricular innovation, and limiting our ability to adopt new pedagogical techniques. We cannot fulfill the promise of integrated science education if we lack sufficient faculty capacity.

*Important note on Nanomedicine:* Dr. Tian’s departure _does not_ remove the need for additional hires. Our plan calls for _two_ collegiate faculty lines in Nanomedicine: one replacement for Dr. Tian and \emph{one new} position to ensure ongoing program growth and resilience.

= Hiring Plan Overview
This hiring plan is in two parts:
  + *Immediate hires* (highest priority):
    - _Nanomedicine Major_: One additional collegiate faculty line (beyond filling the vacancy left by Dr. Tian).
    - _Science, Technology, and Law Minor (STL)_: One collegiate faculty line (Assistant Collegiate Professor or Assistant Professor of Practice).
  + *Potential near-future hires*: 
    - Behavior and Decision Sciences (BDS): One additional faculty line to support both the expanding minor and a potential professional master’s program.

Background research is underway to determine whether a professional master’s program in BDS will proceed. If it moves forward, this additional line will be critical to ensure sufficient coverage and program quality. 

