= Teaching Needs
== Teaching Loads
AIS courses are taught by a mixture of tenure-line faculty and collegiate faculty from both AIS and other departments. Faculty in other departments typically receive course buyouts from AIS for their teaching contributions. Collegiate faculty whose academic home is AIS normally carry a 2-2 load (two courses per semester).

= Teaching Releases
Teaching releases are granted only to those holding AIS leadership roles (for example, division leaders). Historically, these positions have often been filled by tenure-line faculty from other departments. Recently, Dr. Jill Sundie received a release because she joined in January; she is scheduled for a full load in the next academic year.

== Overload Pay
We have offered overload pay on only a few occasions:
  + Drs. Tian, Banerjee, and Kraikivski each received overload pay to cover for Dr. Finkielstein during her research leave (\$9,000 total).
  + Dr. Craig Tollins was compensated for teaching an additional ISC lab (\$9,000).
    \item We regularly pay Daniel Cockrum as an adjunct (\$20,000) to help teach ISC courses.

== Instructional Needs
*Nanomedicine:*

The Nanomedicine major has shown continuous enrollment growth. Two main faculty have covered upper-division Nanomedicine coursework: Dr. Carla Finkielstein (Department of Biological Sciences) and Dr. Chixia Tian (AIS). With Dr. Tian’s departure, we face a significant gap in course coverage, especially for lab-based and medically oriented topics. While Drs. Anand Banerjee and Pavel Kraikivski can provide some support, their theoretical backgrounds limit their ability to teach the more experimental courses focusing on the fundamental science underlying nanotechnologies used in medicine, biotechnology, and pharmaceuticals. 

*Science, Technology, and Law (STL).*

The STL minor has grown in popularity, yet James Steiner, J.D., is the sole full-time faculty member devoted to it, assisted only by an adjunct (Daniel Cockrum). The program is thus vulnerable to any single-person contingency and has limited capacity to expand. Future opportunities include offering more Intellectual Property, Trademark, Entertainment Law, and Name-Image-Likeness (NIL) law courses. These expansions would support students heading to law school or IP-related careers and strengthen our partnerships with other colleges.

*Impact of Separations*
With Dr. Tian leaving, only Dr. Finkielstein remains for upper-division Nanomedicine instruction. Because Dr. Finkielstein’s appointment resides in the Department of Biological Sciences—and no formal MOU currently exists guaranteeing her continued teaching in AIS—the program is vulnerable if her departmental obligations shift or if she chooses not to continue her AIS commitments. This underscores the need for \emph{two} AIS-based collegiate faculty lines in Nanomedicine.

= Specific Requests
== 1. Nanomedicine (Highest Priority)
We request \textbf{one new Assistant/Associate Collegiate Professor} in addition to the replacement of Dr. Tian’s line. The Nanomedicine major’s sustained growth, the field’s rapidly evolving knowledge base, and the need for stronger lab-based and clinical teaching demand a stable faculty team. Ideally, the new hire would also maintain disciplinary or educational links with the Fralin Biomedical Research Institute, enhancing research and internship opportunities for students.

== 2. Science, Technology, and Law (Highest Priority)

We request *one new Assistant Collegiate Professor or Assistant Professor of Practice* to expand STL’s offerings and safeguard continuity of instruction. Possible new courses include Trademark Law, Entertainment Law, and Name-Image-Likeness (NIL) topics, all of which have significant student interest. The extension of the STL minor and the 3+3 initiative with George Mason University’s law school would benefit from this additional line.

== 3. Behavior and Decision Sciences
We recently hired Dr. Jill Sundie to lead a BDS minor and help develop the groundwork for a proposed professional master’s degree. A *second AIS-based Collegiate Faculty* member is essential for:
  - Delivering a consistent rotation of existing undergraduate courses (BDS 2005, BDS 2006, ECON/BDS 3134, and the BDS Capstone),
  - Reducing reliance on other departments for core coverage, 
  - Coordinating prospective employer engagement, internship opportunities, graduate recruiting, admissions, and capstone mentorship for a potential professional master’s program.

If the program proceeds, having two core collegiate faculty in AIS will ensure sustainable delivery.

